<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Megalopolis</h1><p class="st-meta">368 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.) · capital Megalopolis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">'Great City' of Arcadia, founded as a bulwark against Sparta, home of the statesman Philopoemen and the historian Polybius.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Megalopolis was founded between 371 and 368 BCE as the capital of the new <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/arcadian-league">Arcadian League</a>, by moving the people of around forty villages of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadia">Arcadia</a> into a single city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helisson">Helisson</a> river. Its purpose was to block Spartan routes north and to give the Arcadians a shared centre.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The city was laid out on a grand scale with walls of more than 8 kilometres, the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thersilion">Thersilion</a>, a hall for the federal assembly. Its population never filled the walls. Megalopolis was consistently hostile to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sparta">Sparta</a> and pro-Macedonian. Its tyrant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydiadas">Lydiadas</a> voluntarily laid down power and took it into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League</a> in 235 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-iii">Cleomenes III of Sparta</a> sacked it in 223 BCE, but it was rebuilt and produced the Achaean leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philopoemen">Philopoemen</a> and the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>. It lost independence after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaean-war">Achaean War</a> of 146 BCE and declined under Roman rule; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> called it a great desert.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/megalopolis.jpg" alt="Megalopolis" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Adam Voiland · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2014_NASA_Earth_Observatory_image_of_Pearl_River_Delta.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>368 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Megalopolis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Lydiadas</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek (Arcadian)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Megale Polis</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-257&amp;focus=megalopolis" aria-label="Megalopolis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5233 831 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Megalopolis lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5553 1087L5553 1087M5553 1087L5549 1060"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5553 1087h0M5549 1060h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5553 1087h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5553 1087h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Megalopolis. <a href="/atlas?year=-257&amp;focus=megalopolis">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

